On Jul 14, 6:24 pm, "Joseph N. Hall" <remid0d...@gmail.com> wrote:
> By and large, scarcity is not a good thing. I can argue convincingly
> that farming encourages scarcity, because it requires designers to
> make important items that can be generated randomly *more* scarce than
> they would be without that random means of generation.

I am sorry, but you have not convinced me.  And it would take a lot of
convincing to overturn in my head a basic premise of game design.

Your Angband example does not apply to Nethack, because:
1. Your problem with Morgoth could have been a matter of faulty
tactics, not a lack.  If you're going to spend time looking for a
specific artifact in a game with tons, well, then naturally it'll take
a very long time.  Meanwhile, we have no way of knowing that Morgoth
was not beatable in some other fashion.
2. That game is Angband, which despite superficial similarities is not
really that similar to Nethack.
3. The candles referred to down-thread are a special case because they
are the only item that Nethack really demands.  Yet they are generated
randomly, they can be wished for, they can be polypiled for, and I've
never had an occaision where enough weren't generated in Minetown's
guaranteed lighting store.  Every other non-unique item can be done
without.  It might be ill-advised to go too far without magic
resistance, or disintegration resistance or reflection, but it can be
done in a pinch, and those things each have multiple sources.  There
are more than one way to do practically anything.

The right solution to fixing situations where certain items are needed
is to introduce other ways to do it, or make it so there are
alternatives to what the items are needed to do.

